From: Nikolaos Gkarlis <nickgarlis@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 2 Apr 2026 20:14:32 +0200
Subject: rtnetlink: add missing netlink_ns_capable() check for peer netns
Patch-mainline: v7.0
Git-commit: 7b735ef81286007794a227ce2539419479c02a5f
References: CVE-2026-31692 bsc#1267741

rtnl_newlink() lacks a CAP_NET_ADMIN capability check on the peer
network namespace when creating paired devices (veth, vxcan,
netkit). This allows an unprivileged user with a user namespace
to create interfaces in arbitrary network namespaces, including
init_net.

Add a netlink_ns_capable() check for CAP_NET_ADMIN in the peer
namespace before allowing device creation to proceed.

SLE: without the extensive RTNL newlink code rework, we have to move the
new capability check into driver specific newlink code for each of the
drivers. The check needs a pointer to netlink message skb which we cannot
pass as an argument of the ->newlink() callback (kABI). Make it a union
with ->newlink_skb() callback having the extra argument and add a bool
newlink_has_skb which distinguishes which of the two is to be called.
The part around rtnl_link_register() is needed to work around a caveat in
ISO C norm: while it guarantees that struct members omitted in struct
initializer will be zero initialized, it does not do so for holes and
padding. Therefore drivers compiled against original struct rtnl_link_ops
declaration might, in theory, have non-zero newlink_has_skb (even if
highly unlikely in practice). (Actually, we cannot even be sure if the
structure would be initialized in the usual way in an out of tree driver.)

Fixes: 81adee47dfb6 ("net: Support specifying the network namespace upon device creation.")
Signed-off-by: Nikolaos Gkarlis <nickgarlis@gmail.com>
Reviewed-by: Kuniyuki Iwashima <kuniyu@google.com>
Link: https://patch.msgid.link/20260402181432.4126920-1-nickgarlis@gmail.com
Signed-off-by: Jakub Kicinski <kuba@kernel.org>
Acked-by: Michal Kubecek <mkubecek@suse.cz>
Reviewed-by: Petr Pavlu <petr.pavlu@suse.com>

